      "writtenOn": "2023-04-17T11:02:35Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Doesn\u0027t swiotlb on Gunyah differ from \"normal\" memory in the sense that it is shared with the host? AFAIU, those pages would be R/W-able by the host from the start of the boot process of the guest kernel, which differs drastically from pKVM; is that correct? If that\u0027s the case are we assuming that the swiotlb driver is going to take ownership of that subregion, preventing the kernel from using those pages for any other purpose?\n\nIf we are, how do we guarantee that the guest kernel doesn\u0027t make use of those pages during the window between early boot when it parses `/memory` and the point where the driver registers its `\u003creg\u003e` region as reserved? \n\nFurthermore, if we can somehow guarantee that the guest kernel driver registering the swiotlb happens in such a way that the window described above doesn\u0027t exist, adding the swiotlb (shared) region to `/memory` means that the integrity of the guest on Gunyah will depend on the driver being present, which is far from obvious; for example, assuming a guest that doesn\u0027t need virtio (e.g. because it has direct access to real hardware devices), we could shrink the guest kernel by removing that support, introducing a fundamental vulnerability on Gunyah.",
      "parentUuid": "215c6247_c2784a41",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 11,
        "startChar": 54,
        "endLine": 11,
        "endChar": 65
      },
      "revId": "8b9050ede38cbf1650b51548c7604b9e76a8814b",
